When charges move to form a current, magnetic interaction also appears. The following experiment distinguishes this interaction from the electric interaction of stationary charges described above.

Take two flexible conducting wires, place them vertically, and connect them to opposite terminals of a power supply (Figure 233). Their other ends are not connected, so the circuit is open and no steady current flows in the wires;

no clear attraction or repulsion is observed.

Connect the other ends with a wire to close the circuit, so the currents in the two wires run in opposite directions (Figure 234). In this experiment, the adjacent, parallel current-carrying portions repel each other.

Connect the circuit so that the currents in the two wires run in the same direction (Figure 235), and the adjacent, parallel current-carrying portions attract each other. This comparison concerns those parallel portions in the experiment;

it does not say that current direction alone determines the force on every possible shape of complete circuit.

In the seventh-grade physics course, you learned that magnetic phenomena are related to the motion of charges;

an electric current is directed motion of charges. The interaction between the current-carrying wires in this experiment is therefore **magnetic interaction**.

The force on a current-carrying wire in this interaction is called **magnetic force**. Distinguish it from the electric interaction of stationary charges: the magnetic interaction here changes with the currents in the wires.

